  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the line $x=-2$ passes through the points $G(-2,0)$ and $H(-2,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $G$ and $H$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${G}^{\u2032}(2,-6)$ and ${H}^{\u2032}(6,-4).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{\u2032}$ and ${B}^{\u2032}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{-4-(-6)}{6-2}(x-2) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ 2y+12 & =x-2 \\\\ 12+2 & =x-2y \\\\ x-2y & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$",
